verdict: holds
confidence: medium

claim: m4 fixed. The original protection is restored, and the own-page guard covers libc code that runs during the write.
referent:
- install reads each target page's protection from `/proc/self/maps` (`page_prot`) before any change and records it in `Detour::prot_`. Every write, at install and at uninstall, restores exactly that. Test: a hook on an `r--` page leaves it `r--p` after install and after uninstall.
- A page that was already W+X is put back as it was found. The header's W^X paragraph now says so.
- The write path no longer calls libc `memcpy`. It uses its own volatile byte loop, `copy_code`.
- `shares_engine_page` is now `noinline`, so the return address really is in `install`. It also covers `copy_code`, `reseal`, `protect`, libc `mprotect`, `__errno_location` and the active seam. Test: `install(&::mprotect)` returns UnsafePage; the old code returned FlowEnds.
checked: red 3 failures; green passes.
verdict: holds. The guard is still a sanity check, not a proof. It checks the entry address of each function only.
confidence: high for the protection restore, medium for the guard.

### Remaining limits after this round

- Real SELinux execmod and a real two-mapping span (the patch crossing into a mapping with different protection) were not exercised. The two-page reseal path is covered by code reading only.
- A failed install can now return ProtectFailed with `installed() == true`, when the rollback's own `mprotect` was refused. Callers must check `installed()`, not only the status. This is documented at `HookStatus::ProtectFailed`.
- Leaked trampoline pages are never reclaimed. Each one costs a page per abandoned hook.
- m1 (the decoder accepts about 199k architecturally undefined encodings) and m5 (`kp_machotest` unity-includes `sha256.cpp`) were not in scope and are unchanged.
- Everything under "Residual proof boundary" above still applies: no Darwin runtime, no retail hook, not thread-safe.
// x86_64 inline detour: a 14-byte absolute jump at the target, the displaced
// instructions relocated into a trampoline that jumps back.
//
// Same contract as the i386 Detour in src/hooks/trampoline.hpp and the Thumb
// one in src/hooks/arm32/trampoline.hpp -- install/uninstall/original() mean
// the same thing -- but self-contained. It includes neither of those headers
// nor src/platform/platform.hpp, because kp_hooks is built -m32 and this
// engine is 64-bit only.
//
// PATCH. `FF 25 00000000` followed by the 8-byte destination: jmp [rip+0].
// It reaches any address, so the detour can live anywhere. The price is 14
// stolen bytes where i386 needs 5.
//
// TRAMPOLINE. Stolen instructions are copied and re-aimed (decode.hpp's
// relocate). A RIP-relative operand or rel32 branch keeps pointing at the
// same absolute address only if the trampoline sits within +/-2GiB of that
// address, so the trampoline page is mapped near the target with
// MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE hints. The search is centred on the target, not on the
// referents; a referent that is near the target's reach limit can refuse
// (OutOfRange) even when some other page would have fitted. The jump back
// uses the same 14-byte form. A relocated displacement that does not fit
// refuses; nothing is written.
//
// PLAN LIMITS. The walk stops at ret, traps, E9 and FF /4 (refused when they
// end before 14 bytes). A call (E8, FF /2) followed by NOP padding inside the
// window also refuses (FlowEnds): that is the tail of a noreturn wrapper and
// the bytes after the padding belong to the next function. int3 padding
// already refuses. Not detected: a noreturn call followed directly by the
// next function with no padding, and jumps from elsewhere into bytes 1..13.
//
// W^X. The engine never makes a page writable and executable at once. The
// protection the target page(s) had at install is read from /proc/self/maps
// and recorded; the page goes <recorded> -> RW -> <recorded> around each
// write, at install and at uninstall. A page that was already W+X (a JIT
// arena, say) is put back as it was found; the engine does not create one.
// The trampoline page is filled RW and then sealed RX.
//
// Because the target page is briefly not executable, no code that runs
// during the write may share it. install refuses (UnsafePage) when the
// target page holds the engine's write path, libc's mprotect or
// __errno_location, the mprotect test hook, or the caller of install. That
// is a sanity guard, not a proof: callers must not hook a function that
// shares a page with anything another thread may be executing.
//
// FAILED RESTORE. If the kernel refuses to restore the protection after the
// write (SELinux execmod, for example), the engine makes the page RW again,
// writes back the bytes that were there before, and retries the restore.
// It never falls back to RWX. Outcomes, all reported as ProtectFailed with a
// detail string:
//   * rolled back and resealed: memory is as it was before the call;
//   * rolled back, reseal refused again: the old bytes are in place but the
//     page stays RW, not executable. Real execmod denials land here, since
//     the page is already a modified private copy. Treat that page as dead;
//   * rollback refused too: the new bytes stay. For install that means the
//     patch is live, so install returns ProtectFailed with installed() true
//     and keeps the trampoline; uninstall() later restores the recorded
//     protection. For uninstall it means the original bytes are back, so the
//     Detour is uninstalled.
//
// LIFETIME. If the destructor or move-assignment finds the hook still
// installed and uninstall() fails (PatchClobbered: another hook was chained
// on top; ProtectFailed), the trampoline page is leaked on purpose. Code
// that still reaches our detour, directly or through the other hook, calls
// original(); unmapping it would crash that call. leaked_trampoline_pages()
// counts these.
//
// Linux only. Not thread-safe: install/uninstall assume nothing else is
// executing the patched bytes.
#pragma once

#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>

#include "hooks/x64/decode.hpp"

namespace kp::hooks::x64 {

inline constexpr std::size_t kPatchSize = 14;
inline constexpr std::size_t kMaxStolen = kPatchSize + kMaxInsnLen - 1;

enum class HookStatus {
    Ok,
    BadArgs,
    AlreadyInstalled,
    NotInstalled,
    ByteMismatch,     // caller's expected bytes differ from the target
    DecodeFailed,     // an instruction in the stolen range refused
    BranchIntoPatch,  // a stolen instruction names an address inside the stolen range
    FlowEnds,         // ret/jmp before kPatchSize bytes: the next bytes may not be code
    OutOfRange,       // a relocated disp32/rel32 does not fit
    AllocFailed,      // no trampoline page (none free within +/-2GiB when relocation needs one)
    ProtectFailed,    // see FAILED RESTORE: installed() may still be true after install
    UnsafePage,       // the patcher's own code shares the target page
    PatchClobbered,   // uninstall found something other than our patch
};

const char* to_string(HookStatus);

// Plans the stolen range without touching memory: how many bytes, and whether
// any instruction needs relocation. Exposed so tests can run it on synthetic
// prologues at any address.
struct Plan {
    std::size_t stolen = 0;
    bool needs_near = false;  // some instruction is RIP-relative or rel32
    HookStatus status = HookStatus::Ok;
    const char* detail = nullptr;
};
Plan plan(const std::uint8_t* code, std::size_t avail, std::uint64_t at);

class Detour {
public:
    Detour() = default;
    ~Detour();
    Detour(const Detour&) = delete;
    Detour& operator=(const Detour&) = delete;
    Detour(Detour&&) noexcept;
    Detour& operator=(Detour&&) noexcept;

    // Patch `target` to jump to `detour`. If `expect` is given, the target's
    // first expect_len bytes must match it or nothing is written.
    HookStatus install(void* target, void* detour,
                       const std::uint8_t* expect = nullptr,
                       std::size_t expect_len = 0,
                       const char** detail = nullptr);

    // Restores the original bytes and the recorded protection after checking
    // our patch is still there. On failure, installed() says whether the
    // patch is still in place (see FAILED RESTORE).
    HookStatus uninstall(const char** detail = nullptr);

    // Calls the unmodified function. Do not cache it across uninstall.
    void* original() const { return trampoline_; }
    bool installed() const { return installed_; }
    std::size_t stolen_bytes() const { return stolen_; }
    void* trampoline_page() const { return page_; }

private:
    void release();
    void abandon();  // uninstall if needed; leak the trampoline if that fails

    std::uint8_t* target_ = nullptr;
    void* trampoline_ = nullptr;
    void* page_ = nullptr;
    std::size_t page_size_ = 0;
    std::uint8_t original_[kMaxStolen] = {};
    std::uint8_t patch_[kPatchSize] = {};
    std::size_t stolen_ = 0;
    int prot_[2] = {};  // protection of the first and last target page at install
    bool installed_ = false;
};

// Trampoline pages this process has deliberately not unmapped. See ~Detour.
std::size_t leaked_trampoline_pages();

namespace testing {
// Replaces mprotect inside the engine. nullptr restores the real one. Test
// use only: lets the self-test make the kernel "refuse" a protection change.
using ProtectFn = int (*)(void*, std::size_t, int);
void set_protect_hook(ProtectFn);
}  // namespace testing

}  // namespace kp::hooks::x64
// x86_64 inline detour. See trampoline.hpp for the patch shape and W^X rules.
#include "hooks/x64/trampoline.hpp"

#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <unistd.h>

#include <cerrno>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstring>
#include <utility>

namespace kp::hooks::x64 {
namespace {

std::size_t g_leaked = 0;
testing::ProtectFn g_protect = nullptr;

int protect(void* addr, std::size_t len, int prot) {
    return g_protect ? g_protect(addr, len, prot) : mprotect(addr, len, prot);
}

// jmp [rip+0] ; .quad destination
void write_abs_jmp(std::uint8_t* at, std::uint64_t destination) {
    at[0] = 0xff;
    at[1] = 0x25;
    std::memset(at + 2, 0, 4);
    std::memcpy(at + 6, &destination, 8);
}

std::size_t page_size() {
    static const std::size_t size = std::size_t(sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE));
    return size;
}

std::uintptr_t page_down(std::uintptr_t a) { return a & ~(std::uintptr_t(page_size()) - 1); }

// The pages [addr, addr+len) touches: one, or two when it crosses a boundary.
struct Span {
    std::uintptr_t lo = 0;
    std::size_t pages = 0;
    void* base() const { return reinterpret_cast<void*>(lo); }
    std::size_t bytes() const { return pages * page_size(); }
};
Span span_of(const std::uint8_t* addr, std::size_t len) {
    const auto a = reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(addr);
    Span s;
    s.lo = page_down(a);
    s.pages = (page_down(a + len - 1) - s.lo) / page_size() + 1;
    return s;
}

// PROT_* of the mapping that contains `page`, from /proc/self/maps; -1 when
// it is not listed. Runs before any protection changes.
int page_prot(std::uintptr_t page) {
    FILE* f = std::fopen("/proc/self/maps", "re");
    if (!f) return -1;
    char line[512];
    bool line_start = true;
    int prot = -1;
    while (std::fgets(line, sizeof line, f)) {
        const bool parse = line_start;
        line_start = std::strchr(line, '\n') != nullptr;  // long paths span several reads
        if (!parse) continue;
        unsigned long lo = 0, hi = 0;
        char perms[5] = {};
        if (std::sscanf(line, "%lx-%lx %4s", &lo, &hi, perms) == 3 && page >= lo && page < hi) {
            prot = (perms[0] == 'r' ? PROT_READ : 0) | (perms[1] == 'w' ? PROT_WRITE : 0) |
                   (perms[2] == 'x' ? PROT_EXEC : 0);
            break;
        }
    }
    std::fclose(f);
    return prot;
}

// Byte copy that stays in this function: write_code runs it while the
// target page is writable and not executable, so it must not call into
// libc (whose memcpy could share a hooked page). volatile keeps the compiler
// from turning the loop back into a memcpy call.
__attribute__((noinline)) void copy_code(std::uint8_t* dst, const std::uint8_t* src, std::size_t n) {
    auto* d = reinterpret_cast<volatile std::uint8_t*>(dst);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) d[i] = src[i];
}

// Puts each page of `s` back to its recorded protection.
bool reseal(const Span& s, const int prot[2]) {
    if (s.pages == 1 || prot[0] == prot[1]) return protect(s.base(), s.bytes(), prot[0]) == 0;
    bool ok = true;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.pages; ++i)
        ok = protect(reinterpret_cast<void*>(s.lo + i * page_size()), page_size(), prot[i]) == 0 && ok;
    return ok;
}

const char* restore_refused(bool eacces, int outcome) {
    static const char* const msgs[3][2] = {
        {"mprotect restore refused; previous bytes rolled back and page resealed",
         "mprotect restore refused (EACCES: SELinux execmod?); previous bytes rolled back and page resealed"},
        {"mprotect restore refused; previous bytes rolled back but the page is left RW, not executable",
         "mprotect restore refused (EACCES: SELinux execmod?); previous bytes rolled back but the page is "
         "left RW, not executable"},
        {"mprotect restore refused and rollback refused; new bytes left in place",
         "mprotect restore refused (EACCES: SELinux execmod?) and rollback refused; new bytes left in place"},
    };
    return msgs[outcome][eacces ? 1 : 0];
}

// Makes the pages under [addr, addr+len) RW (never executable while
// writable), copies `bytes`, and puts back the recorded protection `prot`.
// If that restore is refused, rolls back to the previous bytes while the
// page can still be written, and tries the restore again. On return,
// `now_new` says whether memory holds `bytes` (true) or what was there
// before (false). See FAILED RESTORE in trampoline.hpp.
HookStatus write_code(std::uint8_t* addr, const std::uint8_t* bytes, std::size_t len, const int prot[2],
                      bool& now_new, const char** detail) {
    now_new = false;
    const Span s = span_of(addr, len);
    std::uint8_t previous[kMaxStolen];
    std::memcpy(previous, addr, len);  // page still has its recorded protection here
    if (protect(s.base(), s.bytes(), PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE) != 0) {
        reseal(s, prot);  // a two-mapping span can be half changed
        if (detail) *detail = "mprotect RW refused; nothing written";
        return HookStatus::ProtectFailed;
    }
    copy_code(addr, bytes, len);
    if (reseal(s, prot)) {
        __builtin___clear_cache(reinterpret_cast<char*>(addr), reinterpret_cast<char*>(addr + len));
        now_new = true;
        return HookStatus::Ok;
    }
    const bool eacces = errno == EACCES;
    // reseal may have restored one page of two; make the whole span writable
    // again before copying back.
    if (protect(s.base(), s.bytes(), PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE) != 0) {
        now_new = true;
        if (detail) *detail = restore_refused(eacces, 2);
        return HookStatus::ProtectFailed;
    }
    copy_code(addr, previous, len);
    const bool sealed = reseal(s, prot);
    if (sealed) __builtin___clear_cache(reinterpret_cast<char*>(addr), reinterpret_cast<char*>(addr + len));
    if (detail) *detail = restore_refused(eacces, sealed ? 0 : 1);
    return HookStatus::ProtectFailed;
}

// True when code that runs while [addr, addr+len) is RW shares one of its
// pages. Not inlined, so the return address below is inside Detour::install.
__attribute__((noinline)) bool shares_engine_page(const std::uint8_t* addr, std::size_t len) {
    const std::uintptr_t engine[] = {
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&write_code),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&copy_code),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&reseal),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&protect),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&restore_refused),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&span_of),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&page_size),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&page_down),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&write_abs_jmp),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&plan),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&decode),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&relocate),
        // libc code called between the flip to RW and the restore.
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&::mprotect),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&::__errno_location),
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(g_protect),
        // Detour::install itself, via the return address of this call.
        reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(__builtin_return_address(0)),
    };
    const Span s = span_of(addr, len);
    for (auto e : engine)
        if (e && e >= s.lo && e < s.lo + s.bytes()) return true;
    return false;
}

// Maps one RW page within +/-2GiB of `near`, trying hints outward in 1MiB
// steps. Returns nullptr if none is free.
void* map_near(std::uintptr_t near, std::size_t size) {
    const std::uintptr_t step = 1u << 20, reach = 0x7ff00000u;
    const std::uintptr_t origin = near & ~(step - 1);
    for (std::uintptr_t d = 0; d < reach; d += step) {
        for (int sign = 0; sign < 2; ++sign) {
            if (d == 0 && sign) continue;
            std::uintptr_t hint;
            if (sign == 0) {
                if (origin > UINTPTR_MAX - d) continue;
                hint = origin + d;
            } else {
                if (origin < d + step) continue;
                hint = origin - d;
            }
            if (hint >= 0x0000800000000000ull) continue;  // user half only
            void* p = mmap(reinterpret_cast<void*>(hint), size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                           MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, -1, 0);
            if (p == MAP_FAILED) continue;
            if (reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(p) != hint) {  // kernel without FIXED_NOREPLACE
                munmap(p, size);
                continue;
            }
            return p;
        }
    }
    return nullptr;
}

}  // namespace

std::size_t leaked_trampoline_pages() { return g_leaked; }
void testing::set_protect_hook(testing::ProtectFn fn) { g_protect = fn; }

const char* to_string(HookStatus s) {
    switch (s) {
        case HookStatus::Ok: return "Ok";
        case HookStatus::BadArgs: return "BadArgs";
        case HookStatus::AlreadyInstalled: return "AlreadyInstalled";
        case HookStatus::NotInstalled: return "NotInstalled";
        case HookStatus::ByteMismatch: return "ByteMismatch";
        case HookStatus::DecodeFailed: return "DecodeFailed";
        case HookStatus::BranchIntoPatch: return "BranchIntoPatch";
        case HookStatus::FlowEnds: return "FlowEnds";
        case HookStatus::OutOfRange: return "OutOfRange";
        case HookStatus::AllocFailed: return "AllocFailed";
        case HookStatus::ProtectFailed: return "ProtectFailed";
        case HookStatus::UnsafePage: return "UnsafePage";
        case HookStatus::PatchClobbered: return "PatchClobbered";
    }
    return "?";
}

Plan plan(const std::uint8_t* code, std::size_t avail, std::uint64_t at) {
    Plan p;
    if (!code) { p.status = HookStatus::BadArgs; return p; }
    std::size_t off = 0;
    while (off < kPatchSize) {
        const auto insn = decode(code + off, avail - off);
        if (!insn.ok()) {
            p.status = HookStatus::DecodeFailed;
            p.detail = insn.refusal;
            return p;
        }
        if (insn.rel != Rel::None) p.needs_near = true;
        off += insn.len;
        if (insn.is_call && off < kPatchSize) {
            const auto next = decode(code + off, avail - off);
            if (next.ok() && next.is_nop) {
                p.status = HookStatus::FlowEnds;
                p.detail = "call followed by NOP padding inside the patch window (noreturn callee?)";
                return p;
            }
        }
        if (insn.ends_flow && off < kPatchSize) {
            p.status = HookStatus::FlowEnds;
            p.detail = "unconditional jump before the patch window is filled";
            return p;
        }
    }
    // Second pass, now the stolen length is known: anything that names an
    // address inside the replaced bytes would land in the middle of the patch.
    for (std::size_t o = 0; o < off;) {
        const auto insn = decode(code + o, avail - o);
        if (insn.rel != Rel::None) {
            const auto r = referent(code + o, insn, at + o);
            if (r > at && r < at + off) {
                p.status = HookStatus::BranchIntoPatch;
                p.detail = "a stolen instruction names an address inside the stolen range";
                return p;
            }
        }
        o += insn.len;
    }
    p.stolen = off;
    return p;
}

Detour::~Detour() { abandon(); }

Detour::Detour(Detour&& o) noexcept { *this = std::move(o); }

Detour& Detour::operator=(Detour&& o) noexcept {
    if (this == &o) return *this;
    abandon();
    target_ = o.target_;
    trampoline_ = o.trampoline_;
    page_ = o.page_;
    page_size_ = o.page_size_;
    std::memcpy(original_, o.original_, sizeof original_);
    std::memcpy(patch_, o.patch_, sizeof patch_);
    stolen_ = o.stolen_;
    std::memcpy(prot_, o.prot_, sizeof prot_);
    installed_ = o.installed_;
    o.target_ = nullptr;
    o.trampoline_ = o.page_ = nullptr;
    o.page_size_ = o.stolen_ = 0;
    o.installed_ = false;
    return *this;
}

void Detour::release() {
    if (page_) munmap(page_, page_size_);
    page_ = trampoline_ = nullptr;
    page_size_ = 0;
}

void Detour::abandon() {
    if (installed_ && uninstall() != HookStatus::Ok && installed_) {
        // The target still jumps to our detour, directly or through a hook
        // chained on top of ours, and the detour calls original(). Unmapping
        // the trampoline would make that call jump into unmapped memory, so
        // the page is leaked on purpose and counted.
        ++g_leaked;
        page_ = trampoline_ = nullptr;
        page_size_ = 0;
        target_ = nullptr;
        stolen_ = 0;
        installed_ = false;
        return;
    }
    release();
}

HookStatus Detour::install(void* target, void* detour, const std::uint8_t* expect,
                           std::size_t expect_len, const char** detail) {
    if (detail) *detail = nullptr;
    if (installed_) return HookStatus::AlreadyInstalled;
    if (!target || !detour || (expect_len && !expect) || expect_len > kMaxStolen) return HookStatus::BadArgs;
    auto* code = static_cast<std::uint8_t*>(target);
    const auto at = reinterpret_cast<std::uint64_t>(target);

    if (shares_engine_page(code, kMaxStolen)) {
        if (detail) *detail = "the detour engine's own code is on the target page";
        return HookStatus::UnsafePage;
    }
    if (expect && std::memcmp(code, expect, expect_len) != 0) {
        if (detail) *detail = "target bytes differ from the expected prologue";
        return HookStatus::ByteMismatch;
    }
    const auto pl = plan(code, kMaxStolen, at);
    if (pl.status != HookStatus::Ok) {
        if (detail) *detail = pl.detail;
        return pl.status;
    }

    // The protection the target page(s) have now. Every write puts back
    // exactly this, at install and at uninstall.
    int prot[2];
    const Span span = span_of(code, pl.stolen);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
        prot[i] = page_prot(span.lo + (i < span.pages ? i : 0) * page_size());
        if (prot[i] < 0) {
            if (detail) *detail = "target page is not listed in /proc/self/maps";
            return HookStatus::ProtectFailed;
        }
    }

    // Trampoline: relocated instructions, then jmp back. Near the target when
    // anything needs re-aiming; anywhere otherwise.
    const std::size_t size = page_size();
    void* page = pl.needs_near ? map_near(reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(target), size)
                               : mmap(nullptr, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
    if (!page || page == MAP_FAILED) {
        if (detail) *detail = pl.needs_near ? "no free page within +/-2GiB of the target"
                                            : "mmap of the trampoline page failed";
        return HookStatus::AllocFailed;
    }
    auto* tramp = static_cast<std::uint8_t*>(page);
    const auto tramp_at = reinterpret_cast<std::uint64_t>(tramp);
    std::size_t out = 0;
    for (std::size_t o = 0; o < pl.stolen;) {
        const auto insn = decode(code + o, pl.stolen - o);
        if (!insn.ok() || !relocate(code + o, at + o, insn, tramp + out, tramp_at + out)) {
            munmap(page, size);
            if (detail) *detail = insn.ok() ? "relocated displacement does not fit in 32 bits" : insn.refusal;
            return insn.ok() ? HookStatus::OutOfRange : HookStatus::DecodeFailed;
        }
        o += insn.len;
        out += insn.len;
    }
    write_abs_jmp(tramp + out, at + pl.stolen);
    if (protect(page, size, PROT_READ | PROT_EXEC) != 0) {
        munmap(page, size);
        if (detail) *detail = "mprotect RX on the trampoline refused";
        return HookStatus::ProtectFailed;
    }
    __builtin___clear_cache(reinterpret_cast<char*>(tramp), reinterpret_cast<char*>(tramp + out + kPatchSize));

    // Patch: jmp [rip+0] to the detour, the rest of the stolen range int3.
    std::uint8_t patch[kMaxStolen];
    std::memset(patch, 0xcc, sizeof patch);
    write_abs_jmp(patch, reinterpret_cast<std::uint64_t>(detour));
    std::memcpy(original_, code, pl.stolen);
    bool now_patched = false;
    const auto st = write_code(code, patch, pl.stolen, prot, now_patched, detail);
    if (!now_patched) {  // nothing written, or rolled back: the trampoline is unreachable
        munmap(page, size);
        return st;
    }
    // Ok, or a failed restore whose rollback was also refused: either way the
    // patch is live and jumps through the trampoline, so this Detour owns an
    // installed hook (and install reports st).
    std::memcpy(patch_, patch, kPatchSize);
    std::memcpy(prot_, prot, sizeof prot_);
    target_ = code;
    trampoline_ = page_ = page;
    page_size_ = size;
    stolen_ = pl.stolen;
    installed_ = true;
    return st;
}

HookStatus Detour::uninstall(const char** detail) {
    if (detail) *detail = nullptr;
    if (!installed_) return HookStatus::NotInstalled;
    if (std::memcmp(target_, patch_, kPatchSize) != 0) {
        if (detail) *detail = "the target no longer holds our patch (another hook chained on top?)";
        return HookStatus::PatchClobbered;
    }
    bool now_original = false;
    const auto st = write_code(target_, original_, stolen_, prot_, now_original, detail);
    if (!now_original) return st;  // our patch is still in place: still installed
    installed_ = false;
    release();
    target_ = nullptr;
    stolen_ = 0;
    return st;
}

}  // namespace kp::hooks::x64
// x86_64 length decoder. See decode.hpp for scope. The tables below are an
// allowlist: an opcode that is not handled explicitly refuses.
#include "hooks/x64/decode.hpp"

#include <cstring>

namespace kp::hooks::x64 {
namespace {

Insn refuse(const char* why) {
    Insn i;
    i.refusal = why;
    return i;
}

// Operand shapes for the one- and two-byte maps.
enum Shape : std::uint8_t {
    Bad,        // refuse
    None,       // opcode only
    M,          // ModRM
    MI8,        // ModRM + imm8
    MIz,        // ModRM + imm16/32 (66 selects 16)
    I8,         // imm8
    Iz,         // imm16/32
    Grp3b,      // F6: ModRM, imm8 only for /0 and /1
    Grp3v,      // F7: ModRM, immz only for /0 and /1
    MovImm,     // B8+r: immz, or imm64 with REX.W
    Rel32,      // E8/E9, 0F 80..8F
    Enter,      // C8: imm16 + imm8
};

Shape one_byte(std::uint8_t op) {
    if (op < 0x40) {
        switch (op & 7) {
            case 0: case 1: case 2: case 3: return M;
            case 4: return I8;
            case 5: return Iz;
            default: return Bad;  // push/pop seg, daa/das/aaa/aas (and prefixes, handled earlier)
        }
    }
    if (op >= 0x50 && op <= 0x5f) return None;         // push/pop r64
    if (op == 0x63) return M;                           // movsxd
    if (op == 0x68) return Iz;
    if (op == 0x69) return MIz;
    if (op == 0x6a) return I8;
    if (op == 0x6b) return MI8;
    if (op == 0x80 || op == 0x83 || op == 0xc0 || op == 0xc1) return MI8;
    if (op == 0x81) return MIz;
    if (op >= 0x84 && op <= 0x8b) return M;             // test/xchg/mov
    if (op == 0x8d) return M;                           // lea
    if (op == 0x8f) return M;                           // pop r/m (checked /0 below)
    if (op >= 0x90 && op <= 0x99) return None;          // nop/xchg/cwde/cdq
    if (op == 0x9c || op == 0x9d) return None;          // pushf/popf
    if (op == 0xa8) return I8;
    if (op == 0xa9) return Iz;
    if (op >= 0xb0 && op <= 0xb7) return I8;
    if (op >= 0xb8 && op <= 0xbf) return MovImm;
    if (op == 0xc6) return MI8;                         // checked /0 below
    if (op == 0xc7) return MIz;                         // checked /0 below
    if (op == 0xc8) return Enter;
    if (op == 0xc9) return None;                        // leave
    if (op >= 0xd0 && op <= 0xd3) return M;             // shifts
    if (op >= 0xd8 && op <= 0xdf) return M;             // x87
    if (op == 0xe8 || op == 0xe9) return Rel32;
    if (op == 0xf5 || (op >= 0xf8 && op <= 0xfd)) return None;  // cmc, flag ops
    if (op == 0xf6) return Grp3b;
    if (op == 0xf7) return Grp3v;
    if (op == 0xfe || op == 0xff) return M;             // checked reg field below
    return Bad;
}

Shape two_byte(std::uint8_t op) {
    if (op == 0x05) return None;                        // syscall
    if (op == 0x31 || op == 0xa2) return None;          // rdtsc, cpuid
    if (op >= 0x10 && op <= 0x17) return M;             // SSE moves
    if (op >= 0x18 && op <= 0x1f) return M;             // prefetch/hint nop/endbr
    if (op >= 0x28 && op <= 0x2f) return M;
    if (op == 0x38) return M;                           // 0F 38 xx: third byte handled by caller
    if (op == 0x3a) return MI8;                         // 0F 3A xx: third byte handled by caller
    if (op >= 0x40 && op <= 0x4f) return M;             // cmovcc
    if (op >= 0x50 && op <= 0x6f) return M;
    if (op >= 0x70 && op <= 0x73) return MI8;           // pshuf*, shift-by-imm groups
    if (op >= 0x74 && op <= 0x76) return M;
    if (op >= 0x7e && op <= 0x7f) return M;
    if (op >= 0x80 && op <= 0x8f) return Rel32;         // jcc rel32
    if (op >= 0x90 && op <= 0x9f) return M;             // setcc
    if (op == 0xa3 || op == 0xab || op == 0xb3 || op == 0xbb) return M;  // bt*
    if (op == 0xa4 || op == 0xac) return MI8;           // shld/shrd imm
    if (op == 0xa5 || op == 0xad || op == 0xaf) return M;
    if (op == 0xb0 || op == 0xb1) return M;             // cmpxchg
    if (op >= 0xb6 && op <= 0xb7) return M;             // movzx
    if (op == 0xba) return MI8;                         // bt* imm
    if (op >= 0xbc && op <= 0xbf) return M;             // bsf/bsr/movsx
    if (op == 0xc0 || op == 0xc1) return M;             // xadd
    if (op == 0xc2 || (op >= 0xc4 && op <= 0xc6)) return MI8;
    if (op >= 0xc8 && op <= 0xcf) return None;          // bswap
    if (op >= 0xd0 && op <= 0xfe) return M;             // SSE2 integer ops
    return Bad;
}

std::int32_t read_i32(const std::uint8_t* p) {
    std::int32_t v;
    std::memcpy(&v, p, 4);
    return v;
}

}  // namespace

Insn decode(const std::uint8_t* code, std::size_t avail) {
    if (!code) return refuse("null code pointer");
    const std::size_t limit = avail < kMaxInsnLen ? avail : kMaxInsnLen;
    std::size_t at = 0;
    bool opsize = false, rex_w = false, rex_b = false, rep = false;
    auto need = [&](std::size_t n) { return at + n <= limit; };

    // Legacy prefixes, any order.
    for (;;) {
        if (!need(1)) return refuse(avail >= kMaxInsnLen ? "instruction longer than 15 bytes" : "truncated");
        const auto b = code[at];
        if (b == 0x66) { opsize = true; ++at; continue; }
        if (b == 0xf3) rep = true;
        if (b == 0xf2 || b == 0xf3 || b == 0xf0 || b == 0x2e || b == 0x36 ||
            b == 0x3e || b == 0x26 || b == 0x64 || b == 0x65) { ++at; continue; }
        if (b == 0x67) return refuse("address-size prefix 67");
        break;
    }
    // REX must immediately precede the opcode.
    if ((code[at] & 0xf0) == 0x40) {
        rex_w = (code[at] & 8) != 0;
        rex_b = (code[at] & 1) != 0;
        ++at;
        if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
        const auto b = code[at];
        if ((b & 0xf0) == 0x40 || b == 0x66 || b == 0x67 || b == 0xf2 || b == 0xf3 || b == 0xf0 ||
            b == 0x2e || b == 0x36 || b == 0x3e || b == 0x26 || b == 0x64 || b == 0x65)
            return refuse("REX not immediately before the opcode");
    }
    if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
    const auto op = code[at++];
    if (op == 0xc4 || op == 0xc5 || op == 0x62 || op == 0x8f) {
        // 8F is POP r/m unless it is an XOP escape; XOP's ModRM always has
        // reg != 0, which the POP /0 check below also refuses.
        if (op != 0x8f) return refuse("VEX/EVEX encoding");
    }

    Shape shape;
    bool two = false;
    std::uint8_t op2 = 0;
    if (op == 0x0f) {
        if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
        op2 = code[at++];
        two = true;
        shape = two_byte(op2);
        if (op2 == 0x38 || op2 == 0x3a) {
            if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
            ++at;  // third opcode byte; ModRM (and imm8 for 3A) follow
        }
    } else {
        shape = one_byte(op);
    }
    if (shape == Bad) {
        switch (op) {
            case 0xeb: case 0xe0: case 0xe1: case 0xe2: case 0xe3:
                return refuse("short branch (rel8)");
            case 0xc2: case 0xc3: case 0xca: case 0xcb: case 0xcf:
                return refuse("return");
            case 0xcc: case 0xcd: case 0xf1: case 0xf4:
                return refuse("trap or halt");
            default:
                if (op >= 0x70 && op <= 0x7f) return refuse("short branch (rel8)");
                return refuse(two ? "unsupported two-byte opcode" : "unsupported opcode");
        }
    }

    Insn insn;
    // REX.W takes precedence over 66: with both, the operand is 64 bits and
    // the immediate stays imm32 (e.g. 66 48 05 imm32 is 7 bytes).
    const std::size_t immz = opsize && !rex_w ? 2 : 4;
    // 90 is NOP unless REX.B makes it xchg r8,rax or F3 makes it pause.
    // 0F 1F is the multi-byte NOP. Both are what compilers pad with.
    insn.is_nop = two ? op2 == 0x1f : (op == 0x90 && !rex_b && !rep);
    std::size_t imm = 0;
    switch (shape) {
        case None: break;
        case I8: imm = 1; break;
        case Iz: imm = immz; break;
        case MovImm: imm = rex_w ? 8 : immz; break;
        case Enter: imm = 3; break;
        case Rel32:
            if (opsize) return refuse("operand-size prefix on a rel32 branch");
            insn.rel = Rel::Rel32;
            insn.rel_offset = std::uint8_t(at);
            imm = 4;
            if (!two && op == 0xe9) insn.ends_flow = true;
            if (!two && op == 0xe8) insn.is_call = true;
            break;
        default: {
            // ModRM forms.
            if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
            const auto modrm = code[at++];
            const unsigned mod = modrm >> 6, reg = (modrm >> 3) & 7, rm = modrm & 7;
            if (!two) {
                if ((op == 0x8f || op == 0xc6 || op == 0xc7) && reg != 0)
                    return refuse(op == 0xc7 && modrm == 0xf8 ? "xbegin" : "unsupported group encoding");
                if (op == 0xfe && reg > 1) return refuse("unsupported group encoding");
                if (op == 0xff) {
                    if (reg == 3 || reg == 5 || reg == 7) return refuse("far transfer or invalid FF encoding");
                    if (reg == 4) insn.ends_flow = true;
                    if (reg == 2) insn.is_call = true;
                }
                if (op == 0x8d && mod == 3) return refuse("lea with register operand");
            }
            if (mod != 3) {
                if (rm == 4) {
                    if (!need(1)) return refuse("truncated");
                    const auto sib = code[at++];
                    if (mod == 0 && (sib & 7) == 5) {  // no base: absolute disp32
                        if (!need(4)) return refuse("truncated");
                        at += 4;
                    }
                } else if (mod == 0 && rm == 5) {      // RIP-relative
                    insn.rel = Rel::RipDisp32;
                    insn.rel_offset = std::uint8_t(at);
                    if (!need(4)) return refuse("truncated");
                    at += 4;
                }
                if (mod == 1) at += 1;
                if (mod == 2) at += 4;
                if (at > limit) return refuse("truncated");
            }
            if (shape == MI8) imm = 1;
            else if (shape == MIz) imm = immz;
            else if (shape == Grp3b) imm = reg <= 1 ? 1 : 0;
            else if (shape == Grp3v) imm = reg <= 1 ? immz : 0;
        }
    }
    if (!need(imm)) return refuse(avail >= kMaxInsnLen ? "instruction longer than 15 bytes" : "truncated");
    at += imm;
    insn.len = std::uint8_t(at);
    insn.refusal = nullptr;
    return insn;
}

std::uint64_t referent(const std::uint8_t* code, const Insn& insn, std::uint64_t at) {
    if (insn.rel == Rel::None) return 0;
    return at + insn.len + std::uint64_t(std::int64_t(read_i32(code + insn.rel_offset)));
}

bool relocate(const std::uint8_t* src, std::uint64_t src_at, const Insn& insn,
              std::uint8_t* dst, std::uint64_t dst_at) {
    if (!insn.ok()) return false;
    if (insn.rel == Rel::None) {
        std::memcpy(dst, src, insn.len);
        return true;
    }
    const std::uint64_t target = referent(src, insn, src_at);
    const std::int64_t disp = std::int64_t(target - (dst_at + insn.len));
    if (disp < INT32_MIN || disp > INT32_MAX) return false;
    const std::int32_t d32 = std::int32_t(disp);
    std::memcpy(dst, src, insn.len);
    std::memcpy(dst + insn.rel_offset, &d32, 4);
    return true;
}

}  // namespace kp::hooks::x64

